+% Venue-edition summary of Sections "Learning a Judge from Sparse Ratings" and "Human-Rating Evidence"; the full sections are in the arXiv edition, as Rule 89 requires for overflow.
+\section{Learning and Human-Rating Evidence}
+\label{sec:case-summary}
+In a synthetic learning-curve study with four targets, a CE-trained softmax judge has significantly lower held-out RPS than a Pearson-trained one from 12 ratings whenever the conditional mean is informative, and correlation training wins only where the mean is nearly constant (up to 96 ratings).
+On public multi-annotator sets, raw CE training cleared Pearson training after $4$ to $64$ ratings on ChaosNLI, DICES, HelpSteer2 and KonIQ, whereas on SummEval Pearson stayed ahead until pseudocounts and calibration were added; full results and intervals are in the arXiv edition.
